From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunshineinabox <aqemail@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 13:30:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] interconnect: qcom: sm6115: mark the QoS ports AP-owned

The driver describes the QoS of 21 BIMC and SNoC ports, with the same
ports, priorities and urgent forwarding as Qualcomm's bengal driver in
the Air X's stock 5.15 kernel (qnoc-bengal.ko, whose 22nd port is UFS),
but marks none of them ap_owned. icc-rpm programs QoS registers only
for ap_owned nodes, so the table was never written and the ports kept
what the boot chain left. Read back on a MANGMI Air X:

  BIMC  apps_proc  BKE on, priority 0         (table: fixed, 0)
        qnm_gpu    BKE off                    (table: fixed, 0)
        tcu_0      BKE off                    (table: fixed, 6)
  SNoC  every port default priority 0         (table: 2 to 4)
        camera_nrt, venus_cpu, pimem, crypto
                   urgent forwarding on       (table: off)

So display and camera requests had no more priority than SD, USB or
QUP traffic, and SMMU table walks none over the GPU.

Mark every port with a QoS description ap_owned, as qcm2290.c does for
the same NoCs and as Qualcomm's 4.19 bus driver marked them. icc-rpm
then writes the registers at probe with the SNoC interface clocks on.
The stock 5.15 driver programs a port only after its first bandwidth
vote, and its BIMC writes pass regmap_update_bits() the mask and value
swapped, so there a priority of 0 writes nothing.

As on qcm2290, AP-owned nodes no longer send their own master bandwidth
to the RPM (apps_proc, snoc_bimc, crypto, qup0, ipa, sdc1, sdc2); the
bus clocks are still voted from the aggregated bandwidth of every node.

Tested on a MANGMI Air X: every register matches the table after boot
and after three suspends to vmin. With the GPU and four big cores both
copying, the GPU got 2.80-2.91 GB/s against 2.77-2.78 before and the
cores 5.8-6.0 against 5.4-5.7; SD (85-88 MB/s) and eMMC reads unchanged.

Signed-off-by: sunshineinabox <aqemail@gmail.com>
